            "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brandenburger_Tor_morgens.jpg",
            "fame": 86,
            "description": {
                "fr": "La porte de Brandebourg, qui se situe à l'entrée de l'ancien Berlin, est un symbole de la ville, mais fut pendant presque trois décennies le symbole de la division de la ville: le monument faisait partie intégrante du mur de Berlin. En 1806, elle est emportée par Napoléon Bonaparte qui veut l'installer à Paris. Après la chute du Premier Empire, le quadrige retourne à Berlin, où il est restauré et agrémenté d'un nouveau symbole de pouvoir.",
                "en": "The Brandenburg Gate is an 18th-century neoclassical monument in Berlin, Germany. Though he had designed many Neoclassical buildings, this was his first significant work in the Greek style, and his last major one; by 1792 he had designed a small neo-Gothic building for the New Garden in Potsdam. The gate was the first element of a \"new Athens on the river Spree\" by Langhans. The gate was originally called \"Friedenstor\" and unceremoniously opened to traffic in August 1791 before its construction had been completed."
            }
