            "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Ruins_of_Djemila_in_S%C3%A9tif,_Algeria.jpg",
            "fame": 56,
            "description": {
                "fr": "Djemila, anciennement Cuicul, est une cité antique située sur le territoire de la commune éponyme de Djemila, dans la wilaya de Sétif, en Algérie. Le site de Djemila abrite les vestiges de l'antique Cuicul, cité romaine, classée patrimoine mondial par l'Unesco.",
                "en": "Djémila, formerly Cuicul, is a small mountain village in Algeria, near the northern coast east of Algiers, where some of the best preserved Roman ruins in North Africa are found. It is in the region bordering the Constantinois and Petite Kabylie. In 1982 Djémila became a UNESCO World Heritage Site for its unique adaptation of Roman architecture to a mountain environment. Significant buildings in ancient Cuicul include a theatre, two fora, temples, basilicas, arches, streets, and houses."
            }
