                "fr": "Le Haʻamonga ʻa Maui est un trilithe situé au royaume des Tonga, près du village de Niutoua, dans le nord de l'île de Tongatapu. D'après les légendes, le Haʻamonga aurait été bâti par le demi-dieu Māui, puisque les pierres seraient trop lourdes pour être transportées par des mortels.",
                "en": "Haʻamonga ʻa Maui is a stone trilithon located in Tonga, on the eastern part of the island of Tongatapu, in the village of Niutōua, in Heketā. It was built in honor of the king's two sons, who are represented by the two upright stones, and their bond represented by the lintel stone on top. The monument and its surrounding areas were declared a protected national park in 1972. Some believe it's a marking to point the direction of pulotu the place of the gods in ancient Tongan legends."
